{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1772908098,"nonce":"ol9M0DtW7LH9GCLp5NxPiTRVcRY=","message":"Root cause clarification from user: the status model is wrong.\n\nCurrent flow: pending → approved (on button press) → [auth-merge runs] → completed\nCorrect flow: pending → executing (on button press, UI locked) → completed (on successful auth-merge exit 0)\n\n\"approved\" as a status conflates user intent with authentication outcome. The user pressing Approve only means they *intend* to approve — it does not mean authentication succeeded. The YubiKey touch, signing, and merge all happen after. Until those complete, the request should be in a locked intermediate state that prevents further user interaction with the auth queue, not \"approved.\"\n\nThis is both a state machine bug and a UX bug. Fixing it would also eliminate the double-approval race entirely.","files":null}]}